rices were going down and  um  so you didn't see what was happening  and then that's actually the reason why  as japan if if we quickly uh moved to to  capital flows briefly japan was creating  all this money  uh via the asset markets  uh inflating asset prices and some of  that spilled over as capital outflows  that explains this flood of money and i  solved that puzzle you know this flood  of money from japan was this bankrupt  creation in asset markets now normally  you'd see that  and you say well they're just printing  money and they're buying foreign assets  the yen should go down but it's not a  it's not an automatic mechanism you know  market players have to understand this  and realize oh they're just printing the  money uh that's why we're gonna sell the  yen and they start to sell the yen but  this didn't happen they saw oh consumer  price inflation zero or negative well  the yen they're obviously not printing  money and the yen should be strong and  it stays strong while they went around  the world buying up assets everywhere  um now you mentioned interest rates of  course that's it's an interesting  question always because any central bank  watcher um  i mean in the mainstream will focus on  interest rates and central banks  themselves will always  try to get your attention  focused on interest rates  and it's a bit like the magician on  stage when they're performing a trick  and of course they don't want you to see  what really does the trick when they do  that hand movement they will distract  your attention with the other hand and  say look here this is happening maybe  have a little diversion story  and that's what interest rates are  but it's such a huge diversion it's it's  worthwhile spending a few uh moments on  this because actually famously there's  so many different schools in economics  right different schools of thought  different economies they all have  different ideas they always argue and  squabble well  there's one  idea that all the different schools of  economic thought of the last 200 years  in english language economics  agree on  namely they all say if you lower rates  this is good for the economy growth will  go up if you raise interest rates this  will slow the economy  you can go back to david ricardo  classical economist alfred marshall  neoclassical economics  keynes um certainly said that  monetarists  said that um  [Music]  you know all sorts of neoclass new  classical economics um  neo-vixilin all the schools of thought  agree on this point and central bankers  for the last 30 years have been saying  this on a daily basis watch interest  rates because when we raise them it's  going to slow things if we lower them  it's going to stimulate  they say this constantly now  because this is repeated so many times  we all assume  quite reasonably that of course this has  been empirically tested and checked  hundreds of times maybe thousands of  times so we don't need to check this  right  well how many empirical studies exist  that demonstrate this relationship  five  zero wow there isn't one because  i did the first empirical check of this  it's very hard to publish this nobody  you know the economist don't want you to  see this but  um  what i found is it's not true  it's it's just not true there is no  empirical evidence for this  so the official so what we're doing  today in the states when we're  increasing rates to prevent from  inflation taking place  that makes zero sense to do it's not  going to help it it's not going to help  if the real cause of inflation which is  not interest rate is still in place and  that is money creation for  unproductive purposes for consumption if  they keep doing that and you can do that  at higher rates you can still push out  the  the credit creation in the banking  system you will get accelerating  inflation and in fact then you'll find  that the higher interest rates make  certain things more expensive mortgages  and whatever rents will start to move up  you know it's not gonna end inflation if  the credit creation continues so i did i  but i wanted to show this because you  can always have scenarios where maybe  you know you think it could work or not  so it's an empirical question  and i did the first empirical test using  data on the us the uk germany and japan  for half a century  and it was open-ended my my question was  simply what actually does the data tell  us is the relationship between interest  rates and we tried together with a  colleague um  [Music]  econometrician  and we um we asked well  looking at three different types of  interest rates short rates um long-term  10-year government bond yields and so on  what is the relation between interest  rates and economic growth  the official story is is a negative  correlation lower rates higher growth  higher rates lower so negative inverse  relationship and causation supposed to  be from interest rates to growth there's  supposed to be some causation there what  we found  was  diametrically opposed to this on both in  both dimensions  the correlation  is actually positive  so higher rates and higher growth always  go hand in hand lower rates and lower  growth always go hand in hand and the  causation statistical causation that you  can test for  runs from growth to interest rates  interest rates follow growth and  therefore they can't be the cause  and that tells you that can't be the the  best monarchy policy tool at all  that paper is published you can also  look it up that's called  re it's also open source reconsidering  monetary policy  um the only journal that will publish  this fairly good journal still but um  was ecological economics it's in there  peer-reviewed journal  an empirical examination of the relation  between interest rates and gdp growth  we've got another paper coming out now  which looks at real growth industrial  production for 19 countries it's the  same conclusion so reality is interest  rates work differently and they're not